{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Broadcasting — php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Broadcasting</h1>
    <p>
        Broadcasting is how php-via pushes updates to multiple clients at once. When a user's action should
        change what <em>everyone</em> sees — not just themselves — you broadcast to a scope.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-basics">The basics</h2>
    <p>
        Call <code>$app-&gt;broadcast(scope)</code> after modifying shared state. Every context registered
        in that scope will re-render its view and push the update via SSE:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$increment = $c->action(function (Context $c) use ($app, $counter): void {
    $newVal = $app->incrementGlobalState('shared_counter');
    $counter->setValue($newVal);

    // Push to everyone on this route
    $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope('/'));
}, 'increment');
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="scope-targets">Scope targets</h2>
    <p>You can broadcast to any scope string:</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
// All connected clients everywhere
$app->broadcast(Scope::GLOBAL);

// All clients on a specific route (a bare Scope::ROUTE would reach every route)
$app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope('/dashboard'));

// A custom scope (e.g. a chat room)
$app->broadcast('room:lobby');

// Equivalent shorthand when inside a context:
$c->broadcast(); // broadcasts to $c's primary scope
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="auto-broadcast">Auto-broadcast on scoped signals</h2>
    <p>
        Scoped signals auto-broadcast when <code>setValue()</code> is called — you don't always need
        an explicit <code>broadcast()</code>. The auto-broadcast fires to the signal's scope:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
// This signal is ROUTE-scoped. setValue() and increment() auto-broadcast to route:/
$shared = $c->signal(0, 'count', Scope::routeScope('/'));

$c->action(function (Context $c) use ($shared): void {
    $shared->increment();
    // No explicit broadcast needed — auto-broadcast fires for Scope::routeScope('/')
}, 'inc');
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        An action that writes several scoped signals, or writes them and also calls <code>broadcast()</code>,
        still re-renders each client once. See the next section.
    </p>

    <h2 id="timing">When the update is sent</h2>
    <p>
        Actions, timers, SSE hooks and broker messages all run in a coroutine. There, <code>broadcast()</code>
        only marks the scope. The worker's next flush re-renders every marked scope once and publishes it to
        other workers and nodes once. A client in several marked scopes renders once. Outside a coroutine, for
        example in a CLI script, and while the worker shuts down, <code>broadcast()</code> renders before it
        returns.
    </p>
    <p>
        A flush runs at the end of the current event-loop turn when the worker's last flush started at least
        one broadcast tick ago and ended at least half a tick ago, so on a quiet server the update goes out at
        once. Otherwise it runs as soon as both have passed, and every broadcast in between shares it. The tick
        is 25&nbsp;ms by default. The re-rendering a worker does per second then depends on its clients and the
        tick, not on how many actions arrive: flushes whose views render for F milliseconds without waiting on
        I/O use at most F / 25 of the worker up to F = 12.5, and F / (F + 12.5) above that. After every flush
        the worker has at least half a tick for requests, timers and I/O, so slow flushes slow requests down
        but cannot starve them. Under load, clients skip intermediate frames and still end on the latest state.
        Broadcasts received from other workers and nodes go through the same flush.
    </p>
    <p>
        Flushes of different scopes run side by side, so a view that waits on a slow database query delays only
        its own scope. A scope broadcast while its fan-out is still running is rendered by the first flush after
        that fan-out ends. When a later broadcast finds a fan-out that has been running for over a second, the
        worker logs a warning naming the scope. When two flushes render the same tab and the one that started
        first finishes last, it renders the tab again, so the tab ends on the newer state after a brief step back.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withBroadcastTickMs(50); // fewer, larger flushes under load; 0 flushes every event-loop turn with no gap
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Each worker counts its flushes in <code>$app-&gt;getStats()-&gt;getBroadcastStats()</code>, also shown
        under <code>broadcast_stats</code> in the dev-mode <code>/_stats</code> endpoint: broadcasts scheduled
        and coalesced, flushes, the last, longest and total flush time, and <code>flush_overruns</code>, the
        flushes that took longer than the tick. Flush times are wall time, including time the views spend
        waiting on I/O while the worker does other work. When the views do not wait on I/O, flushes longer
        than the tick mean the worker spends more than two thirds of its time in them under sustained load,
        so climbing overruns are the signal to raise the tick or make the views cheaper.
    </p>
    <p>What this means for your code:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Views render the state as it is when the fan-out runs. Ten broadcasts in one action send one
            frame with all ten changes, as long as the action does not wait on I/O between them: a database
            call in between lets the flush run and send a half-updated frame. Write first and broadcast
            last.</li>
        <li>If an action sets a value, broadcasts and then changes the value back, clients never see it.
            Call <code>$app-&gt;flushBroadcasts()</code> before changing it back.</li>
        <li>Patches the action queues itself, such as <code>execScript()</code> or <code>$c-&gt;sync()</code>,
            reach the tab before the broadcast's frame. Call <code>flushBroadcasts()</code> first when the
            script needs the new DOM.</li>
        <li>The action's HTTP response does not wait for the fan-out to every client, so a
            <code>data-indicator</code> can clear before the new frame arrives.</li>
        <li>With <code>worker_num &gt; 1</code>, a flush reads each scoped signal from shared memory once and
            reuses the value for every tab it renders. A view that computes a new value from one, such as
            <code>setValue($s-&gt;int() + 1)</code>, computes it from that value, so use <code>increment()</code>
            or <code>mutate()</code> there.</li>
    </ul>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$send = $c->action(function (Context $c) use ($app): void {
    $app->mutateGlobalState('messages', fn (?array $m) => [...$m ?? [], $c->input('text')]);
    $app->broadcast('room:lobby');

    // Render the new message everywhere now, then scroll this tab to it.
    $app->flushBroadcasts();
    $c->execScript('document.querySelector("#messages").lastElementChild.scrollIntoView()');
}, 'send');
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        When another flush is already rendering <code>room:lobby</code>, <code>flushBroadcasts()</code> waits for
        it and then renders the new message. It waits at most one second: past that it logs a warning and
        returns, and the message's frame follows on a later flush, after the script.
    </p>
    <p>
        To render and publish on every call instead, as earlier releases did, turn coalescing off:
        <code>(new Config())-&gt;withBroadcastCoalescing(false)</code>. There is no tick then, except under
        sustained load: a broadcast that finds its scope's fan-out running makes that fan-out render once more
        when it ends, and when other actions keep that going for 8 passes in a row, the rest goes to a flush
        paced by the tick, so the action that started the fan-out can return. These flushes count under
        <code>flushes</code> in <code>broadcast_stats</code>, while <code>scheduled</code> stays 0.
    </p>
    <p>
        In both modes, a view that broadcasts its own scope while it renders, directly or from a coroutine it
        starts, makes its fan-out render again. When it does that in 8 passes in a row, the fan-out stops and
        the worker logs "Broadcast re-entrancy limit reached" with the scope's name.
    </p>

    <h2 id="connect-disconnect">Broadcasting on connect and disconnect</h2>
    <p>
        Use <code>onClientConnect</code> / <code>onClientDisconnect</code> app-level hooks to broadcast
        whenever a client joins or leaves. This replaces polling timers for presence features:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
// Broadcast to the route when occupancy changes — presence counter stays live
$app->onClientConnect(fn(Context $c) => $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope($c->getRoute())));
$app->onClientDisconnect(fn(Context $c) => $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope($c->getRoute())));
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>See <a href="/docs/lifecycle">Lifecycle</a> for full details on connect/disconnect hooks.</p>

    <h2 id="global-state">Combining global state and broadcast</h2>
    <p>
        Use <code>setGlobalState</code> to store shared data in process memory, then broadcast to re-render
        all clients with the new value. The standard multiplayer pattern:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
// Initialize once
$app->setGlobalState('messages', []);

// In an action:
$send = $c->action(function (Context $c) use ($app): void {
    $msg = ['user' => substr($c->getId(), -4), 'text' => $_POST['text'] ?? ''];
    $app->mutateGlobalState('messages', fn (?array $msgs) => array_slice([...$msgs ?? [], $msg], -50)); // keep last 50

    $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope('/chat'));
}, 'send');
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <strong>Multi-worker note.</strong> With <code>withWorkerNum(&gt;1)</code>, GlobalState is backed
        by an <code>OpenSwoole\Table</code> shared across all workers. Values are PHP-serialized
        before storage; the default column size is 32&nbsp;KB per value and keys are at most 63 characters.
        Read-then-write loses updates when two workers do it at once, so append with
        <code>mutateGlobalState()</code> rather than <code>globalState()</code> plus <code>setGlobalState()</code>.
        For large values (deep arrays, long strings), increase the limit with
        <code>Config::withGlobalStateTableSize(int $maxRows, int $maxValueBytes)</code> before calling
        <code>start()</code>. An <code>\OverflowException</code> is thrown at write time if a value
        exceeds the configured size. For high-frequency or large shared data, prefer scoped signals
        with explicit <code>broadcast()</code> over GlobalState.
    </p>

